      'A weather forecast that gives a 30 % chance of a storm on the day of your race is a risk: the storm has not happened, it may not happen, and you can still act — move the start, pack a rain shell, buy event insurance. A sprained ankle the morning of the race is an issue: it has happened, its probability is one, and the only questions left are how bad it is and what you do now. Mixing the two produces bad decisions. Teams that log issues as risks report a “likely” event that is already costing them, and teams that log risks as issues spend money fixing things that may never occur.',
